<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Hardened Runtime entitlements for the NOTARIZED, Developer-ID-distributed Mosh.
         Mosh is NOT sandboxed (it's a direct-download DAW, not a Mac App Store app), so
         everything below is a Hardened Runtime *exception*, not an App Sandbox grant —
         do NOT add App Sandbox keys (com.apple.security.app-sandbox and its
         com.apple.security.network.*/files.* friends) here; they'd be silently inert
         without app-sandbox=true and would misdescribe the app's actual posture to
         anyone auditing this file.

         Applied ONLY to the notarized distribution build, by
         scripts/release/sign-and-notarize.sh (invoked by `./run-mosh.sh release`
         locally and by .github/workflows/release.yml on a tag push). The everyday
         ad-hoc `./run-mosh.sh deploy` / `deploy-anira` paths sign with `codesign
         --sign -` (ad-hoc, no entitlements file, no Hardened Runtime) and never read
         this file — they're for the owner's own Mac, not third-party distribution.

         Every key below is load-bearing (dropping it breaks a shipping feature); every
         *omission* is a deliberate, verified choice, documented at the bottom. Re-audit
         this file whenever plugin hosting, the WebView, or IPC/companion transport
         changes shape — the check in `scripts/release/check-plist-keys.sh` guards the
         Info.plist TCC keys, but nothing automated guards *this* file's completeness;
         it's a human judgment call tied to what the codebase actually does. -->

    <!-- THE essential one for a plugin host. Hardened Runtime's library validation
         otherwise refuses to load any code not signed by our Team ID or Apple — which
         is every third-party VST3/AU the user owns, plus the bundled LibTorch/anira
         dylibs in the real-time-RAVE build (deploy-anira; not built by release.yml
         today, but the entitlement is harmless to carry either way). Without this, no
         user plugin loads — this is the whole point of a plugin-hosting DAW being
         notarized instead of staying ad-hoc-only. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- JIT: the JavaScript engine behind the WebView UI (JUCE's WKWebView-backed
         WebBrowserComponent → WebKit's JavaScriptCore), and any plugin/DSP code that
         emits machine code at runtime (some synths JIT their DSP graphs). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Distinct from allow-jit (which permits MAP_JIT pages): this permits the older
         mprotect(PROT_WRITE|PROT_EXEC) pattern that some legacy VST3 synths still use.
         Kept because Mosh hosts arbitrary third-party plugins picked by the user, not a
         fixed allow-list; dropping it would crash whichever of those plugins relies on
         it, on a user's machine, with no recourse. Do NOT remove thinking allow-jit
         covers it — they gate different code paths in the kernel's page-protection
         checks. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Microphone: native voice control + audio recording (src/voice/NativeSpeech.mm,
         src/audio/*). Pairs with NSMicrophoneUsageDescription (the TCC prompt string;
         already present in the base Info.plist, not injected by
         cmake/InjectInfoPlistKeys.cmake — only the three keys that history has proven
         get silently dropped are handled there; see that file's own header). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Camera: the WebView multiplayer-presence camera feature. Pairs with
         NSCameraUsageDescription, which IS one of the cmake/InjectInfoPlistKeys.cmake
         keys — scripts/release/check-plist-keys.sh asserts it survives signing. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Considered and deliberately EXCLUDED (each re-verified 2026-07-17 against
         the current source tree — see the grep commands in the sign-and-notarize PR
         description / commit for the exact checks run):

         • com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables — would let
           DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES etc. survive into the hardened process. Grepped
           src/ + service/ + run-mosh.sh for DYLD_*: zero hits. The Python service is
           spawned as an independent process (juce::ChildProcess), not via dyld
           injection into Mosh itself, so this is genuinely unused, not just unused
           *yet*. Re-grep before ever adding it back; it's one of the more dangerous
           Hardened Runtime exceptions (arbitrary code injection into the signed
           binary) and notarization visibly frowns on it.

         • com.apple.security.get-task-allow — the "this process is debuggable" flag.
           MUST stay absent (defaults to false when omitted): Apple's notary service
           rejects submissions where it's true. Xcode strips it automatically for
           Release archives; because we hand-build the codesign invocation ourselves
           in sign-and-notarize.sh, there's no Xcode step to rely on — the guarantee
           here is simply that no key by this name appears in this file. Grepped for
           it repo-wide: zero hits outside this comment.

         • com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection — a strictly
           more permissive superset of allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory
           (removes ALL executable-page protection, not just the two known-needed
           patterns). Apple explicitly discourages it. Nothing in the plugin-hosting
           or WebView path needs it beyond what the two narrower entitlements above
           already grant; adding it would be a pure security downgrade for zero
           functional gain.

         • com.apple.security.automation.apple-events — grepped src/ for
           AppleEvent / osascript / NSAppleScript: zero hits. Mosh never sends Apple
           Events to other apps (the iPhone companion is HTTP+mDNS over
           RemoteCompanionServer, not AppleScript/Automator).

         • Any com.apple.security.network.* / com.apple.security.files.* /
           com.apple.security.temporary-exception.* key — these are App Sandbox
           entitlements. They do nothing for a non-sandboxed app (no
           com.apple.security.app-sandbox=true above) and would misrepresent Mosh's
           actual security model to a reader of this file. If Mosh is ever sandboxed
           for Mac App Store distribution, this whole file gets re-derived from
           scratch against the Sandbox's stricter defaults — it is not a smaller
           version of that future file.

         • No XPC services / helper tools / LaunchAgents exist in the bundle (grepped
           cmake/ + CMakeLists.txt + src/ for XPCService / Contents/Helpers /
           LaunchAgent / LaunchDaemon: zero hits), so there is nothing beyond the one
           main executable for sign-and-notarize.sh's inside-out signing loop to sign
           with entitlements. If a helper process is ever added, it needs its OWN
           entitlements (typically a narrower subset — e.g. rarely allow-jit) applied
           at its own signing step, not by inheriting this file wholesale.
         ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
</dict>
</plist>
